<!--
    Workspaces M1 (#54, ADR-001): the per-leaf tab strip is now the REAL MUX
    TabView (Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Controls.TabView), the same control the classic
    per-window tab row uses (see TabRowControl.xaml). It replaces the old
    re-skinned WUX ListView whose classic-TabViewItem chrome — rounded top,
    bottom flare/chamfer, inter-tab separators, hover highlight — was hand-built
    as a model projection (the "RECONCILE BEFORE UPSTREAM PR" brush block,
    deleted). Native TabViewItem chrome / icon / tooltip now render that look.

    The TabView is driven by WorkspaceModel through MANUAL TabView.TabItems()
    projection in TabStripView.cpp (NOT TabItemsSource binding). Selection is a
    one-way projection of the model (the page pushes SelectedItem from the
    ActiveTabChanged diff; a user SelectionChanged is an intent only,
    reentrancy-guarded).

    Drag (Workspaces #55): all three MUX drag flags are FALSE. MUX's built-in tab
    drag arms a Windows SHELL drag (CoreDragOperation) that fails E_ACCESSDENIED in
    this hosting context and escalates to an (uncatchable) 0xc000027b failfast on ANY
    tab drag — the shell tab drag does not work here (even production WT can't drag
    tabs in this environment). So tab REORDER is a CUSTOM in-process pointer gesture
    that never touches CoreDragOperation: pointer handlers on the TabView detect a
    press-then-threshold drag, then the DragOverlay below captures the pointer and
    draws an insertion line; on release the gesture raises MoveTabRequested(tabId,
    dstIdx) and the model diff re-projects (no write-back). CROSS-leaf move (#57) +
    tear-out (#60) are likewise custom. See TabStripView.h/.cpp for the gesture.

    IsAddTabButtonVisible is false in M1: the native "+" → new-tab-in-this-leaf
    wiring is a SHOULD that we DEFER (see the .cpp). The mux namespace + the
    Background="TabViewBackground" mirror TabRowControl.xaml.
-->
<UserControl x:Class="TerminalApp.TabStripView"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:local="using:TerminalApp"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:mux="using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Controls"
             mc:Ignorable="d">

    <Grid>
        <mux:TabView x:Name="TabView"
                     Background="{ThemeResource TabViewBackground}"
                     AllowDropTabs="False"
                     CanDragTabs="False"
                     CanReorderTabs="False"
                     IsAddTabButtonVisible="False"
                     TabWidthMode="SizeToContent" />

        <!--
            Workspaces #55 custom-drag overlay. Sits above the TabView but is
            transparent + Collapsed + non-hit-test, so clicks pass straight through
            to the tabs. Only an active reorder drag turns it Visible + hit-test and
            CAPTURES the pointer on it (NOT the TabViewItem — MUX un-refcounted-
            CapturePointer()s its items internally and would steal/lose our capture;
            TabManagement.cpp:1081). It hosts the insertion-line adorner; both are
            driven entirely from TabStripView.cpp (Canvas.Left / Height / Visibility).
        -->
        <Canvas x:Name="DragOverlay"
                Background="Transparent"
                IsHitTestVisible="False"
                Visibility="Collapsed">
            <Border x:Name="InsertionIndicator"
                    Width="2"
                    Visibility="Collapsed">
                <Border.Background>
                    <SolidColorBrush Color="{StaticResource SystemAccentColor}" />
                </Border.Background>
            </Border>
        </Canvas>
    </Grid>

</UserControl>
